WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
- Did you know that every lawyer
has a huge swinging hammer?

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.480
This is not a metaphor.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.110
John Oliver covered the dispute

00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:10.410
between Jim the Texas Hammer Adler.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.540
- I'm Jim Adler, the Texas Hammer.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.920
- And Mike, the Alabama Hammer Slocumb.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:16.820
- I'm Mike Slocumb, the Alabama Hammer.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:20.030
- To recap, Jim Adler says he
spent more than $100 million

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:22.290
on ads branding himself
as the Texas Hammer,

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:25.197
a slogan he's been using since the 1990s.

00:00:25.197 --> 00:00:26.620
- The Texas Hammer.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:29.090
- He sued Mike Slocumb,
the Alabama Hammer,

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:31.670
for copying his commercials line by line.

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:33.333
- I'm Mike Slocumb, Alabama Hammer.

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:35.690
- Slocumb also calls himself the DC Hammer

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:37.680
for his DC area commercials.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.370
Both advertisements feature the lawyers

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:41.900
battling an 18-wheeler,
holding sledgehammers,

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.460
and speaking right into the camera

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:46.828
promising to beat insurance
companies at their own game.

00:00:46.828 --> 00:00:48.490
(tires screech)

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:49.780
- That's what I thought.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.460
- Slocumb says he stopped running that ad

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:53.820
and also claims that he only
calls himself the Alabama

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:56.750
and DC Hammers and
doesn't mess with Texas.

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:59.287
- I'm Mike Slocumb, the Alabama Hammer.

00:00:59.287 --> 00:01:02.530
- Slocumb did appear to copy
that 18-wheeler commercial

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:05.360
but also has other weapons or tricks

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:09.470
up his lawyer's sleeve
or suit sleeves, I guess.

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:11.950
Here's Slocumb threatening
to shoot insurance companies.

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:14.558
- Yeah, well, here's a
target we can all agree on.

00:01:14.558 --> 00:01:16.420
(gun fires)

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.760
- So what is this dispute really about?

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.430
Well, Adler owns the federal
trademark registrations

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:23.360
for the trademarks the Texas Hammer,

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:27.040
its Spanish translation,
El Martillo Tejano,

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.090
and The Hammer.

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:30.370
- Soy Jim Adler, el Martillo Tejano.

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:31.490
- So what does this mean?

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.930
Well, a trademark is a
word, symbol, or phrase

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:35.870
used to identify a particular manufacturer

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:37.960
or seller's products and distinguish them

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:42.210
from the products of another
or services of another.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:44.040
Nike and adidas have different logos.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.330
That's how you can distinguish
which brands of shoes

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:48.280
you were buying and where they came from.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:49.490
Trademarks are generally considered

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:52.160
a form of intellectual property
like copyrights and patents.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:54.380
However, these are all different concepts.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.520
A patent is a legal right to an invention,

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.840
which is a product or
process that provides

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:01.500
a new way of doing something
or solving a problem.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.130
It usually has some sort of utility value.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:05.990
A patent entitles the patent holder,

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:07.190
who is often the inventor,

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:08.750
to the exclusive right to replicate, use,

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:11.330
or license or sell that thing.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:12.790
Copyright, on the other hand,

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:14.970
protects original works
of creative authorship

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:16.660
fixed in a tangible medium.

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:18.510
Copyright attaches to things like songs,

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:20.730
books, articles, movies, and art.

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:22.520
That means that authors
have the exclusive rights

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:24.880
to do whatever they want to
the things that they make,

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:26.660
the the expression that they make,

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:29.450
including the right to sell
or otherwise profit from them.

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:31.950
But copyrights and trademarks
are very different.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.140
Trademarks don't reward
the trademark holder

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.330
by giving them the
right to sell something.

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:38.380
The person or business
which holds a trademark

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:41.510
has the right to stop
others from using a similar

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:43.970
or identical trademark
without their permission.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:46.610
A purpose of a trademark is
to prevent consumer confusion

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:49.560
about the source of
the goods and services.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.700
Trademarks prohibit any
marks that have a likelihood

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:53.300
of confusion with an existing one.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.400
That means that a business
cannot use a symbol

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:57.820
or brand name if it
looks or sounds similar

00:02:57.820 --> 00:02:58.750
or has a similar meaning

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:00.520
to the one that's already on the books

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:02.780
if it's already being used in commerce,

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:05.430
especially if the products
or services are related.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:06.900
So that's a crash course on the difference

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:09.580
between copyrights,
trademarks and patents,

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:12.450
and believe me, it's way, way
more complicated than that.

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:15.920
But this is the source of dispute
between Adler and Slocumb.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.310
Adler says that Slocumb calls
himself the Texas Hammer

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:21.400
in the Texas area, which
creates consumer confusion

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.840
since Adler has the trademark
to the phrase Texas Hammer.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:27.100
And to prove that someone
infringed on your trademark,

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:28.470
you have to show that it's likely

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:29.670
to cause consumer confusion

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:31.570
about the source of the
products or services.

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:34.170
Adler says Google
searches for Texas Hammer

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:36.220
pull up a phone number
that automatically connects

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:38.430
the searcher to Slocumb's call center.

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:40.810
This is known as click to call technology.

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:42.590
Click to call ads are a new addition

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:43.920
to the search engine advertising,

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.820
and these ads don't
necessarily link to a website,

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:48.410
but instead trigger the user's device

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:50.170
to call a predetermined phone number.

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:52.840
Adler says that by
purchasing these keywords,

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:55.940
Slocumb is trading on the good
will that Adler accumulated

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:58.770
in the Texas market with his
hundreds of millions of dollars

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:01.570
in advertising related
to the Texas Hammer mark.

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:03.740
And while Slocumb's nearly
identical commercial

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:06.700
seems like a pretty clear
cut case of copying,

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:11.040
the issue there would be
potentially copyright infringement,

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.730
because copyright
applies to the expression

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:16.330
of the commercial, the
scenes that are used,

00:04:16.330 --> 00:04:20.240
the lines of dialogue in
that particular commercial.

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:22.070
Whereas trademark would be the issue

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:23.830
when it comes to the
actual marks themselves,

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:26.270
the use of Hammer in advertising,

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:28.110
whether it's the Texas
Hammer, the Alabama Hammer,

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:30.530
or any other kinds of hammers that come up

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:31.970
in that particular commercial.

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:33.360
And it gets even more complicated

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:35.180
when you're talking about
click to call advertising

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:39.060
and advertising on search
engines and online,

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:42.380
where a competitor buys
the right to advertise

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:45.320
using a competitor's mark in a keyword.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:47.750
So for example, if you say
anytime someone searches

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:50.500
for "the Texas Hammer,"
I want you to show my ads

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:53.228
for the Alabama Hammer.

00:04:53.228 --> 00:04:56.530
It's a complicated issue
that I won't go too far

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:59.560
into in this video, but there
is an additional wrinkle

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:01.640
to this case that makes it much more fun,

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:03.610
because when Adler
applied for his original

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:07.020
Hammer trademarks, he told the US PTO,

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:09.160
the US patent and trademark office,

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:10.860
that he wouldn't be confused with lawyers

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:13.970
in other jurisdictions such
as the Kentucky Hammer.

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:16.720
That's because he needed to
establish the geographic scope

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:18.320
of his trademark protection.

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:20.530
Just because you have
a trademark in one area

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:22.760
doesn't mean you can exclude everyone else

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:24.610
from using that mark
in the entire country.

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:25.790
Now, John Oliver does a great job

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:28.120
of covering the controversy
between Slocumb and Adler,

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:29.500
which is hilarious in its own right,

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:31.690
but what Oliver misses out on is that

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:35.040
this is just the tip
of the Hammer iceberg.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:39.360
There are far far more Hammer
lawyers in the United States.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:40.830
It's kind of its own thing.

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:43.370
So let's talk about the Kentucky Hammer.

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:45.480
- I'm attorney Darryl Issacs, the Hammer.

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:48.300
- That is Darryl Issacs, a Kentucky, Ohio,

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:51.730
and Indiana lawyer who
bills himself as the Hammer.

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:53.360
Like Slocumb and Adler,

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:56.310
Issacs is also an auto
accident personal injury lawyer

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:58.590
who likes to fight with 18-wheelers.

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:00.890
So if you search for "the Hammer lawyer,"

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:04.300
Google may display an ad
for the Hammer Law Firm,

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:07.940
which is actually Isaacs,
neither Adler or Slocumb.

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:09.890
So if Adler spent a
hundred million dollars

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:12.790
on advertisements, you
can imagine how much money

00:06:12.790 --> 00:06:14.070
the Kentucky Hammer has spent

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:16.500
on his own Superbowl commercials.

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:19.040
One of those ads shows
Isaacs in a spaceship

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:22.740
reenacting "Star Wars,"
which raises all kinds

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:25.550
of other intellectual property issues.

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:27.750
But it's pretty funny because
when Princess Leia's spaceship

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:30.370
gets T-boned, she knows just who to call.

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:32.870
- Help me, Darryl Issacs,
you're my only hope.

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:33.870
- On my way.

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:37.000
- You know, for lawyers, they
are skirting awfully close

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:40.300
to very established intellectual property.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:42.020
Issacs also rode a dragon

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:44.070
for his next Superbowl advertisement,

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:45.890
which was based on "Game of Thrones."

00:06:45.890 --> 00:06:47.770
In fairness though, that is better CGI

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:49.660
than you can find on the CW.

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:51.900
And that advertisement is better

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:54.190
than the last episode
of "Game of Thrones."

00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:55.850
Now, in addition to
calling himself the Hammer

00:06:55.850 --> 00:06:58.000
in the Louisville and
Indianapolis markets,

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:00.470
Issacs markets himself
as the heavy hitter.

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:02.120
But things get even more complicated

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:04.760
because would you imagine
that there might be more

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:07.730
than three hammering lawyers in the USA?

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:11.790
Well, of course there are
because Georgia has Jamie Casino,

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:13.920
a man so committed to his Hammer persona,

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:16.240
he ran several commercials
during the Superbowl

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:18.150
in his local market of Savannah.

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:20.910
The 2014 version is a two
minute long commercial

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:22.950
and features Jamie wearing black leather,

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:24.210
getting personal with the hammer

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:27.630
while a Metallica cover band
plays in the background.

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:29.860
I mean, just look at that fire.

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:31.790
And here's Jamie with
a cross around his neck

00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:35.910
absolutely messing up some
TVs in the 2018 Superbowl ad.

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:37.360
Is he taking on insurance companies

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:40.480
or preparing to stake some vampires?

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:41.313
Who can say?

00:07:41.313 --> 00:07:42.730
But we're not done yet.

00:07:42.730 --> 00:07:44.350
Insurance companies are also the target

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:46.700
of the Illinois Hammer, Bradley Dworkin.

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:50.240
- My name is Bradley
Dworkin, the Illinois Hammer.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:51.950
- Now my favorite part
of the Illinois Hammer ad

00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:53.820
is this depiction of the
insurance company lawyer

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:57.360
as this fat slob with a messy
desk and slovenly clothing.

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:58.490
If you've ever had to interact

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:00.940
with insurance company lawyers,

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:02.893
you'll get a kick out of that.

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:06.193
It's not exactly inaccurate.

00:08:07.039 --> 00:08:08.890
Can you tell that I've had to sue

00:08:08.890 --> 00:08:11.440
a number of insurance companies before?

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:13.780
Yeah, come at me,
insurance company lawyers.

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:16.670
Now the Illinois Hammer's most
recent commercial is animated

00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:18.940
and contains a truly memorable jingle.

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:21.550
♪ Dial 777, all sevens today ♪

00:08:21.550 --> 00:08:24.480
♪ The Illinois Hammer is on the way ♪

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:25.710
- Honestly, I don't hate it,

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:28.302
but could there be yet more Hammers?

00:08:28.302 --> 00:08:29.353
Well, of course there are.

00:08:29.353 --> 00:08:30.186
We're not nearly done yet,

00:08:30.186 --> 00:08:31.740
because lawyers are not that creative,

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:33.610
and the hammer just sounds cool.

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:36.320
So Lowell the Hammer Stanley is a Virginia

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:38.320
personal injury lawyer whose mantra is

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:41.270
I am the hammer, they are the nails.

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:43.050
Now there is nothing subtle

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:44.890
about this particular Hammer's messaging.

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:47.000
His basic message is size matters.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:49.100
And it says so right on the screen.

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:51.983
- Lowell the Hammer Stanley, 459-CASH.

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:56.330
- Frankly, the New Mexico
Hammer's commercial is tame

00:08:56.330 --> 00:09:00.310
by comparison, New Mexico
hammer attorney Brett Parnell,

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:04.150
I guess he just didn't feel
like investing in CGI zombies,

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:05.450
but you get the point across.

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:07.350
You need a lawyer with a big hammer.

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:09.680
And honestly, there are hundreds
of other hammers out there,

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:10.590
because there are a lot of lawyers

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:12.520
who have Hammer as their last name.

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:14.940
So what about lawyers
with the surname Hammer,

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:17.130
like St. Louis based attorney Mark Hammer?

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:18.650
Well, Hammer is his actual surname,

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:20.780
so he can't be enjoined from using

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:22.660
all the hammer puns he wants,

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:25.160
but Mark Hammer's website
doesn't use any of them.

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:26.560
This could be because Mark Hammer

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:28.520
isn't down to ride the dragon,

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:31.250
or perhaps it's because
Missouri's ethical limitations

00:09:31.250 --> 00:09:33.750
on attorney advertisements
are generally more restrictive

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:35.650
than the ABA's recommended rules.

00:09:35.650 --> 00:09:37.660
Of course, men aren't the
only ones with big hammers.

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:38.900
Amberley Hammer brings it down

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:41.760
on doctors who commit medical malpractice.

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:45.060
So how could it be that there
are so many hammer lawyers

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:46.430
in the United States?

00:09:46.430 --> 00:09:48.680
Well, under US trademark law,
there's no inherent problem

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:51.430
with several different lawyers
using the term The Hammer

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:53.470
in descriptive geographical terms.

00:09:53.470 --> 00:09:55.760
A trademark is all about
preventing consumer confusion.

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:57.660
So if you are providing goods and services

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:00.260
to the entire United
States, then your trademark

00:10:00.260 --> 00:10:02.180
would probably cover the
entire United States.

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:05.450
But lawyers are a classic
type of service provider

00:10:05.450 --> 00:10:08.370
that are limited to certain
geographical location.

00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:09.450
Passing the bar in one state

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:12.300
generally doesn't allow you
to practice in another state.

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:15.420
So often lawyers are
limited geographically

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:18.560
by the states where they
are licensed to practice.

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:20.620
And when a person applies for a trademark

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:22.290
with the US patent and trademark office,

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:23.580
they obtain the rights of the trademark

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:26.330
either by being the first person
to use the mark in commerce

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:28.300
or being the first to register the mark.

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:29.980
And that's what Adler was
claiming when he registered

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:31.570
the phrase "the Texas Hammer."

00:10:31.570 --> 00:10:33.520
He claims that he beat
others, including Slocumb,

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:35.920
to the punch by using the mark first

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:37.690
in a given geographical area.

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:39.330
Using the mark in commerce
usually means that

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:41.700
you have sold a product
or service to the public

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:43.060
with that mark attached to it.

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:45.210
If you were the first to do
this, you'll have priority

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:47.350
to use that mark in the geographic area

00:10:47.350 --> 00:10:48.540
where the sales took place,

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:50.560
as well as other geographic areas

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:52.770
where your business would
be expected to expand

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:55.030
or where the reputation of
your mark is established.

00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:56.970
So that's why Adler's
filings say that his mark

00:10:56.970 --> 00:10:59.970
won't infringe on what
Issacs is doing in Kentucky.

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:01.750
Adler has no intention
of running commercials

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:03.830
branding himself as the Kentucky Hammer.

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:06.020
- Call the Hammer today.

00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:07.550
- But at the same time, it's hard to talk

00:11:07.550 --> 00:11:09.980
about attorney advertisements
without talking about

00:11:09.980 --> 00:11:12.090
the regulation of those advertisements.

00:11:12.090 --> 00:11:13.780
Because depending on what state you're in,

00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:15.730
lawyers aren't allowed
to say whatever they want

00:11:15.730 --> 00:11:17.820
to advertise and solicit clients.

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:21.230
And Jim the Hammer Shapiro
is probably the most infamous

00:11:21.230 --> 00:11:24.878
Hammer of all, because his
antics were so notorious

00:11:24.878 --> 00:11:27.500
that New York actually created new rules

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:29.940
to stop him from making
obnoxious commercials.

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:33.050
- I cannot rip out the
hearts of those who hurt you.

00:11:33.050 --> 00:11:35.870
I cannot hand you their severed heads,

00:11:35.870 --> 00:11:40.220
but I can hunt them down
and settle the score.

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:42.360
- Shapiro's aggressive commercials ran in

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:44.980
the Rochester, New York
and south Florida areas,

00:11:44.980 --> 00:11:47.590
in addition to popping
up in Winnipeg, Canada.

00:11:47.590 --> 00:11:49.880
Now New York eventually
suspended his license

00:11:49.880 --> 00:11:51.530
for running misleading commercials

00:11:51.530 --> 00:11:54.560
and sending a solicitation
letter to a comatose patient.

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:56.540
We conclude that any
reasonable attorney would know

00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:58.580
that a solicitation letter
sent to a hospitalized

00:11:58.580 --> 00:12:01.140
comatose patient in the
days immediately following

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:03.540
a collision between her
automobile and a train

00:12:03.540 --> 00:12:05.330
would reach the patient and her family

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:06.520
at a time when they were unable

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:09.350
to exercise reasonable judgment
in retaining an attorney.

00:12:09.350 --> 00:12:11.680
Respondent, who had actual
knowledge of the condition

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:14.540
of the accident victim,
will not be heard to argue

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:16.300
that the disciplinary rule required him

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:20.110
to be a, quote, mind and body
reader in order to determine

00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:22.370
whether his solicitation
letter could be sent.

00:12:22.370 --> 00:12:24.560
Lawyers get a reputation
for ambulance chasing,

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:27.980
but actually most states
actually have explicit rules

00:12:27.980 --> 00:12:30.550
that say you cannot do ambulance chasing.

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:33.440
And so it's not surprising
that Shapiro got in trouble.

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:35.660
In 2007, the New York state court system

00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:37.900
adopted new disciplinary
rules that were inspired

00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:39.620
by the Shapiro commercials.

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:42.910
The rules prohibit nicknames
such as The Hammer.

00:12:42.910 --> 00:12:44.800
